<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="phytosterol biosynthesis (plants)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-2541" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Ubiquitous Plant Pathways (UPP) that was generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most land plants. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; Sterols are isoprenoid-derived molecules that have essential functions in eukaryotes. In vertebrates |FRAME: CHOLESTEROL| is by far the major sterol, whereas a mixture of various sterols is present in higher plants, including |FRAME: CPD-4143| (usually predominant), |FRAME: CPD-4162|, |FRAME: CPD-707|, |FRAME: CPD-4127|, |FRAME: CPD-4161| and |FRAME: CHOLESTEROL|. Sterols are present in three forms: free sterols (the major form with a free 3&amp;beta;-hydroxyl), steryl esters, and steryl glucosides. Free sterols are integral components of the membrane lipid bilayer and thus interact with some molecular species of phosphoglycero(sphingo) lipids. The extent of this interaction plays a functional role in the regulation of membrane fluidity and permeability. Sterols have also been shown to play important roles in embryonic development of vertebrates and the formation of membrane microdomains (rafts), which may constitute signaling centers for multiple pathways |CITS:[11278759]|. Finally, sterols are precursors of compounds with a high physiological activity such as brassinosteroids, an important class of plant hormones involved in growth and development |CITS: [8602526]|, ecdysteroids in invertebrates |CITS:[7791536]| and plants |CITS:[Coriot93]|, and antheridiol and oogoniols, sexual pheromones of aquatic fungi belonging to the saprolegniale class |CITS:[1913877]|. &lt;b&gt;In plants:&lt;/b&gt; The plant sterol biosynthesis pathway is essentially linear until reaching 24-methylene lophenol, with a bifurcation downstream of this compound that results in two main pathways leading to 24-methyl- and 24-ethyl-sterols. The simplicity of this pathway is essentially due to i) the relatively high substrate specificity of most enzymes from the postsqualene segment of the biosynthetic chain and ii) to the channeling of substrates/products through microdomains of the endoplasmic reticulum |CITS:[15377227]|. A number of minor sterols have been detected suggesting that in addition to the main sterol biosynthesis pathway depicted here a number of side pathways exist. Those compounds remain largely undetected until sterol biosynthesis inhibitors are used or genes encoding various enzymes of the biosynthesis pathways are mutated. Our knowledge of the sterol biosynthesis pathway in plants results largely from the study of &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt; mutants and through detecting the accumulated intermediates and/or functional complementation of the mutant phenotypes with wild type genes. &lt;b&gt;Key Notes on the Enzymes:&lt;/b&gt; i) Of the many enzymes involved in the sterol biosynthesis pathway, very few have been purified to homogeneity. Nonetheless many genes encoding those proteins have been cloned in |FRAME:TAX-3701| and have been expressed in heterologous systems where the substrate specificity of the corresponding enzymes has been assessed (see individual comments on particular enzymes). ii) One of the major rate-limiting steps of this pathway is the C-24 methylation of |FRAME: CYCLOARTENOL| catalyzed by |FRAME: EC-2.1.1.41| (SMT1). iii) |FRAME: EC-1.14.14.154| (OBT14DM; CYP51) from |FRAME:TAX-4577| (corn) has been described as a major potential herbicide target.</Description>
