    <DataNode elementId="AT2G26260-MONOMER" textLabel="sterol-4-alpha-carboxylate 3-dehydrogenase (decarboxylating)" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="O64845" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="750" centerY="475" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">The bifunctional &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis &lt;/i&gt; protein encoded by gene |FRAME:AT2G26260 3&amp;beta;-HSD/D2| was shown to complement the yeast strain &lt;i&gt;erg26&lt;/i&gt; deficient in &lt;i&gt;Erg26p&lt;/i&gt; which requires ergosterol or cholesterol supplementation for viability |CITS:[16835224]|. &lt;b&gt;Note&lt;/b&gt;: the last seven terminal amino acids of the cloned sequence were replaced with the KKID sequence for efficient targeting of the recombinant protein to the &lt;i&gt;Saccharomyces&lt;/i&gt; ER. The activity of the enzyme was also assessed by partial purification of the recombinant protein in the form of microsomal preparations. NAD&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt; was used a cofactor. A variety of substrates were assayed. It was determined that the enzyme could catalyze both the dehydrogenation and decarboxylation of a number of 3&amp;beta;-hydroxy-4-carboxysterols leading to the formation of their corresponding 3-oxo-C4-decarboxylated products. In addition, it was assessed that the 3-&amp;alpha;-configuration was dehydrogenated although with a 50-fold lower activity than its 3&amp;beta; epimer |CITS:[16835224]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT2G26260-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT2G26260.1" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Smr" value="Q67ZE1" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Pride" value="Q67ZE1" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Interpro" value="IPR003388" />
