    <DataNode elementId="AT2G29390-MONOMER" textLabel="4α-methyl-δ7-sterol-4α-methyl oxidase" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q9ZW22" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="450" centerY="555" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">Several lines of evidence suggest that this enzyme is involved in the second of two demethylation steps that occur during plant sterol biosynthesis |CITS: [14653780]|. It should be capable of performing the reaction: 4&amp;alpha;-methyl-&amp;delta;7-sterol + 3 NADPH + 3H&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt; + 3 O&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; -&gt; a 4&amp;alpha;-carboxy, &amp;delta;-7-sterol + 3 NADP&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt; + 3 H&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;O. Enzymes of this class carry out three successive monooxygenation steps on the methyl group causing it to undergo the following series of changes: CH3-&gt;CH2OH-&gt;CHO-&gt;COOH. The final product of these reactions can then be acted on by a 3&amp;beta;-hydroxysteroid-dehydrogenase/C4-decarboxylase and a 3-keto-reductase to produce the final demethylated product |CITS: [14653780]|. To date, no direct assays of this enzyme&apos;s activities have been performed.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT2G29390-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT2G29390.1" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Uniprot" value="Q9ZW22" />
      <Property key="Catalyzes" value="RXN-26946, RXN-26945, RXN-26944" />
      <Property key="CatalyzesCount" value="3" />
      <Property key="Accession1" value="AT2G29390.1" />
      <Property key="FunctionalAssignmentStatus" value=":PROBABLE-ENZYME" />
      <Property key="Gene" value="AT2G29390" />
      <Property key="InstanceNameTemplate" value="MONOMER-*" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_14653780" />
    </DataNode>
    <DataNode elementId="AT5G50375-MONOMER" textLabel="cycloeucalenol cycloisomerase" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q9M643" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="600" centerY="555" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">In plants, sterol biosynthesis occurs via the cyclization of epoxysqualene to the pentacyclic cycloartenol. Plants, therefore, must isomerizes the cyclopropane ring to form &amp;Delta;&lt;sup&gt;8&lt;/sup&gt; tetracycles present in the tetracyclic sterols. This two-enzyme route differs from sterol biosynthesis in animals and fungi which only require the one-step cyclization of oxidosqualene to the tetracyclic &amp;Delta;&lt;sup&gt;8&lt;/sup&gt;-triterpene lanosterol. In plants, the conversion of the pentacyclic cycloeucalenol to the tetracyclic obtusifoliol is achieved by the enzyme cycloeucalenol cycloisomerase. The &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis&lt;/i&gt; CPI1 gene was cloned and used in recombinant yeast where it was shown to have a cycloeucalenol cycloisomerase activity |CITS:[10788449]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT5G50375-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT5G50375.2" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Prodom" value="PD317471" />
