    <DataNode elementId="AT3G02580-MONOMER" textLabel="Δ⁷-sterol-C5-desaturase" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q39208" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="750" centerY="555" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT3G02580-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT3G02580.1" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Uniprot" value="Q39208" />
      <Property key="Synonym_1" value="C-5 sterol desaturase" />
      <Property key="Synonym_2" value="dwf7" />
      <Property key="Catalyzes" value="RXN-33769, RXN-1F-2550, RXN-5764
TargetP location: _

The &lt;i&gt;STE1&lt;/i&gt; gene has been cloned from &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis&lt;/i&gt; |CITS:[10344195]| and used to complement the mutant phenotype of the &lt;i&gt;ste1&lt;/i&gt; &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis&lt;/i&gt; plants defective in the &amp;Delta;&lt;sup&gt;7&lt;/sup&gt; sterol C-5 desaturation step, as well as that of the yeast &lt;i&gt;&amp;Delta;erg3&lt;/i&gt; strain also defective in &amp;Delta;&lt;sup&gt;7&lt;/sup&gt; sterol C-5 desaturase |CITS:[10344195]|. No purified form of this enzyme has yet been described to assess enzymatic characteristics. The main substrate of STE1 is episterol (found both in &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis&lt;/i&gt; and yeast), however, it is most likely able to convert avenasterol as well. Indeed, the &lt;i&gt;ste1&lt;/i&gt; &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis&lt;/i&gt; mutant is affected in both branches of the sterols biosynthesis pathway and is deficient in both campesterol and sitosterol. Plants expressing the transgenic &lt;i&gt;STE1&lt;/i&gt; gene have wild-type levels of both compounds, showing that the &amp;Delta;&lt;sup&gt;7&lt;/sup&gt; sterol C-5 desaturase activity of STE1 affects both branches of the pathway |CITS:[10344195]|." />
      <Property key="CatalyzesCount" value="3" />
      <Property key="Accession1" value="AT3G02580.1" />
      <Property key="FunctionalAssignmentStatus" value=":PROBABLE-ENZYME" />
      <Property key="Gene" value="AT3G02580" />
      <Property key="InstanceNameTemplate" value="MONOMER-*" />
    </DataNode>
    <DataNode elementId="AT3G52940-MONOMER" textLabel="Δ14-sterol reductase" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q9LDR4" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="150" centerY="635" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">NIL The protein encoded by &lt;i&gt;FK&lt;/i&gt; was shown by epistatic analysis of various sterol biosynthesis &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis&lt;/i&gt; mutants to be a sterol C-14 reductase involved in the conversion of 4&amp;alpha;-methyl-5&amp;alpha;-ergosta-8,14,24(28)-trien-3&amp;beta;-ol to 4&amp;alpha;-methylfecosterol. Structurally, the &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis&lt;/i&gt; FK protein is highly leucine-rich (16.5%) and almost entirely hydrophobic. Hydropathy plot analysis suggests that &lt;i&gt;FK&lt;/i&gt; encodes a membrane protein with eight to nine transmembrane segments and a carboxy-terminal cytosolic tail of about 43 amino acids |CITS:[10859166]|. Although the enzyme has not been purified, its expression in mutant &lt;i&gt;&amp;Delta;erg24&lt;/i&gt; yeast deficient for sterol C-14 reductase complemented the yeast mutant phenotype |CITS:[10859166]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT3G52940-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT3G52940.1" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Arrayexpress" value="Q9LDR4" />
